.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt SYSLOG.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm syslog.conf
.Nd logd compatibility message routing configuration
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
file supplies simple compatibility routes to
.Xr logd 8 ,
the ASL system logger.
Native ASL rules are configured in
.Xr logd.conf 5 .
This interface implements a subset of traditional
.Xr syslogd 8
configuration syntax.
.Pp
Each route has a selector and a destination separated by spaces or tabs.
A selector is a facility, a period, and a severity level.
The levels, from highest to lowest severity, are
.Cm emerg , alert , crit , err , warning , notice , info ,
and
.Cm debug .
A route selects that level and all higher severities.
An equals sign before the level selects only that level:
.Cm daemon.=debug .
The aliases
.Cm panic , error ,
and
.Cm warn
mean
.Cm emerg , err ,
and
.Cm warning ,
respectively.
Other comparison operators are not supported.
.Pp
Facilities are
.Cm auth , authpriv , console , cron , daemon , ftp , kern , lpr ,
.Cm mail , news , ntp , security , syslog , user , uucp , launchd ,
and
.Cm local0
through
.Cm local7 .
Facility and level names are case insensitive.
An asterisk as the facility selects all facilities.
An asterisk as the level selects all severities.
The level
.Cm none
excludes a facility.
The
.Cm mark
facility and periodic mark messages are not supported.
.Pp
Facilities may be separated by commas to share a level.
Selectors may be separated by semicolons to share a destination.
For a matching facility, later selectors override earlier selectors,
including exclusions.
Each route is evaluated independently.
.Ss Program filters
A line beginning with
.Ql !prog
restricts subsequent routes to messages whose ASL Sender is exactly
.Ql prog .
Names are case sensitive.
A comma-separated list, such as
.Ql !prog1,prog2 ,
matches either sender.
The form
.Ql !-prog1,prog2
matches senders other than those listed.
The line
.Ql !*
resets the filter.
The most recent program filter applies until it is replaced or reset.
It does not inspect prefixes in the message body.
In particular, kernel messages retain the sender and PID prefix
.Ql kernel[0]\&:
in BSD text output, consistent with the ASL store.
A kernel message containing a program name in its body does not acquire
that program's Sender.
.Ss Destinations
An absolute pathname appends selected messages to that file.
The special path
.Pa /dev/console
writes to the console, except that kernel messages are not repeated there.
The kernel already writes them to the console.
.Pp
A destination beginning with
.Ql @
forwards selected messages over UDP to the named host.
An optional colon and port select a destination port;
the default service is
.Ql syslog .
An asterisk destination sends selected messages to all logged-in users.
.Pp
The leading
.Ql -
pathname prefix is not supported as synchronization control.
For compatibility it is accepted and stripped, but it does not change
write or synchronization behavior.
File routes do not explicitly call
.Xr fsync 2
after kernel messages.
.Ss Includes and comments
The directive
.Dq include directory
reads non-hidden files ending in
.Pa .conf
from that directory in pathname order.
Included files start with no program filter.
Includes may be nested up to 16 levels below the main file.
Blank lines and lines whose first non-blank character is
.Ql #
are ignored.
A comment may follow the destination when separated by whitespace.
.Ss Unsupported syntax
The legacy
.Ql #!prog
form is a comment, not a program filter.
The
.Ql !+prog
form is not supported; use
.Ql !prog .
Hostname filters, including
.Ql #+hostname ,
.Ql +hostname ,
and their exclusion forms, are not supported.
Forms beginning with
.Ql #
are comments and do not restrict subsequent routes.
Property-based filters, regular expressions, shell pipe destinations,
and lists of individual users are not supported.
Malformed selectors are diagnosed and are not installed as routes.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/syslog.conf -compact
.It Pa /etc/syslog.conf
Compatibility routes for
.Xr logd 8 .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following routes illustrate thresholds, exclusions, exact levels,
facility lists, and program filters:
.Bd -literal
# Errors and higher, except private authentication.
*.err;authpriv.none              /var/log/errors

# All kernel messages, retaining kernel[0]: in text output.
kern.*                          /var/log/kernel

# Only daemon debug messages.
daemon.=debug                   /var/log/daemon.debug

# Mail and news errors and higher.
mail,news.err                   /var/log/spoolerr

# Messages whose Sender is ftpd.
!ftpd
*.*                             /var/log/ftpd

# Messages whose Sender is bird or bird6.
!bird,bird6
local0.info                     /var/log/bird

# Messages from every sender except noisy.
!-noisy
user.notice                     /var/log/quiet

# Reset the Sender filter.
!*
authpriv.*                      /var/log/secure
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Xr logd.conf 5 ,
.Xr logd 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
